 Linux: I have a program that loads another program. The loaded program should be able to call functionality from the loading program and the loading program sends periodic time-critical signals to the loaded program.

What mechanisms should I use for this?

Your design choice depends on whether the loaded program runs in the same process memory space (plugin model) or as a separate process (isolated process model).

Option 1: In-Process Architecture (Plugin Model)

Use this when you need sub-microsecond latency and high-frequency communication.

    Calling Host Functionality: The host loads the dynamic library using dlopen(). Pass a context struct containing host function pointers to the loaded library during its initialization function. Alternatively, build the host with the -rdynamic linker flag so the dynamic library can resolve exported host symbols via dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, "symbol_name").

    Time-Critical Signals:

        Direct Callbacks: Drive timing using a dedicated host thread running a high-resolution timer (timerfd with CLOCK_MONOTONIC) that calls a callback exported by the plugin.

        Lock-Free Ring Buffers: For transferring data alongside time ticks without lock contention, use a Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) ring buffer in shared heap memory.

Option 2: Multi-Process Architecture (Isolated Model)

Use this if the loaded program must be isolated so a crash in the child process does not bring down the main host process.

    Calling Host Functionality:

        Unix Domain Sockets (AF_UNIX): Best for bidirectional command/response function invocation with minimal overhead (~2–5 microseconds).

        Shared Memory (shm_open + mmap): Best for high-throughput zero-copy data sharing between host and child.

    Time-Critical Signals:

        eventfd: A lightweight kernel event counter mechanism that can be polled or read like a file descriptor. It integrates cleanly with epoll loops.

        POSIX Real-Time Signals (SIGRTMIN to SIGRTMAX): Unlike standard Linux signals, real-time signals are queued (so signals aren't lost if fired rapidly) and can carry a small data payload using sigqueue().

        Futexes: Shared-memory locks/events that operate entirely in user space unless a process actually needs to sleep, providing the lowest-latency IPC signal.

Mechanism	Host Calling Method	Signaling Method	Latency	Crash Resilience
In-Process (dlopen)	Function Pointers / dlsym	Direct Callback / timerfd	Sub-microsecond	Low (Child crash kills Host)
Multi-Process (IPC)	Unix Sockets / Shared Memory	eventfd / Real-Time Signals	~1–5 microseconds	High
