I would like to isolate the loaded program (The program is actually a shim that then loads the DLL itself) for crash resistance and security. The calls from the loaded program to the host are not time critical, but the signals from the host to the loaded program are. The host to loaded signals also do not need any payload, as that is done through shared memory

For a multi-process architecture with process isolation, shared-memory data transport, time-critical host-to-child signaling, and non-time-critical child-to-host calls, the optimal Linux stack uses memfd_create, eventfd (or shared futexes), and Unix Domain Sockets.
Recommended Architecture Components
1. Host-to-Child Time-Critical Signaling

Since data is transferred via shared memory, the signal only needs to notify the child that new data is available or a timer tick occurred.

    eventfd (Recommended):

        How it works: A lightweight, kernel-backed 8-byte event counter file descriptor.

        Performance: Very low overhead (~1 microsecond latency). The host triggers a signal with a single 8-byte write(), and the child process unblocks with a read() or monitors it via an epoll event loop.

        Setup: The host creates the eventfd instance and passes the descriptor to the child process via file descriptor inheritance (fork/exec) or over a socket using SCM_RIGHTS.

    Shared Futex / Shared Condition Variable (Lowest Latency Alternative):

        How it works: A pthread_cond_t initialized with the PTHREAD_PROCESS_SHARED attribute placed inside the shared memory region.

        Performance: Sub-microsecond latency. Atomically checked in user space; only triggers a system call (sys_futex) when the child is actively sleeping and needs to be woken up.

2. Data Payload (Shared Memory)

    memfd_create + mmap:

        How it works: Creates an anonymous file in RAM without creating a path on the filesystem (unlike traditional /dev/shm POSIX shared memory).

        Benefits: Highly secure—no other process on the system can discover or access the memory region unless handed the file descriptor.

3. Child-to-Host Function Calls (Non-Time-Critical)

    Unix Domain Sockets (AF_UNIX, SOCK_SEQPACKET):

        How it works: Provides reliable, bi-directional IPC with preserved message boundaries (unlike streaming sockets).

        Benefits: Simple command/response RPC mechanism. Allows the host to easily authenticate the child process using SO_PEERCRED and pass file descriptors (memfd and eventfd) at startup.

Component Overview
Role	Mechanism	Primary Advantage
Time-Critical Signals	eventfd	Low latency, clean integration with epoll, minimal overhead.
Data Transfer	memfd_create + mmap	Zero-copy performance, clean security/isolation footprint.
Non-Critical Calls	AF_UNIX (SOCK_SEQPACKET)	Reliable RPC framing, secure FD passing during setup.
Recommended Initialization Sequence

    Host Setup: Host creates a Unix socket pair (socketpair()), a shared memory descriptor (memfd_create()), and a signal descriptor (eventfd()).

    Spawn Shim: Host forks and executes the child shim process, inheriting or receiving the socket descriptor.

    Descriptor Exchange: Host passes the memfd and eventfd descriptors to the shim over the socket using sendmsg() with SCM_RIGHTS.

    Memory Mapping: Both processes mmap() the memfd payload area.

    Runtime Loop:

        Host writes data to shared memory and calls write() on the eventfd to wake the child.

        Child reads the eventfd, processes the shared memory data, and sends non-time-critical response messages back over the Unix socket as needed.

