Re-iterate on realtime signals.

POSIX Real-Time Signals (SIGRTMIN to SIGRTMAX) are specialized Linux signals designed to overcome the fundamental limitations of standard Unix signals (SIGUSR1, SIGALRM, etc.) for time-critical, low-latency applications.
Key Capabilities vs. Standard Signals

    Guaranteed Signal Queueing: Standard Linux signals use a simple bitmask. If a host sends 10 standard signals before the child processes the first one, 9 are dropped. Real-time signals are backed by a kernel queue (RLIMIT_SIGPENDING) — every signal sent via sigqueue() is delivered.

    Deterministic Delivery Ordering: Real-time signals guarantee delivery order. If multiple distinct RT signals are pending, lower signal numbers take priority (SIGRTMIN executes before SIGRTMIN+1). Multiple instances of the same RT signal are processed strictly FIFO (First In, First Out).

    Payload Support: Sent using sigqueue() instead of kill(), allowing the host to pass a 4-byte/8-byte payload (union sigval containing an integer or void pointer) directly inside the signal notification.

    No Predefined System Behaviors: Standard signals carry default system actions (like termination or dump core). SIGRTMIN through SIGRTMAX (typically 32 distinct signals on Linux) are strictly reserved for application-defined IPC.

Reception Mechanisms in the Loaded Process

To handle incoming real-time signals without running into asynchronous signal-safety issues (like forbidden heap allocations inside handlers), the loaded child process typically uses one of two patterns:
1. Synchronous Signal Waiting (sigwaitinfo / sigtimedwait)

The child process blocks all RT signals, dedicated thread loops calling sigwaitinfo(). This converts incoming asynchronous signals into synchronous event-loop notifications without triggering traditional signal handlers.

    Latency: Extremely low (direct thread wake-up).

    Safety: Zero async-safety issues; thread processes the payload inline inside standard code flow.

2. Asynchronous Signal Handler (sigaction with SA_SIGINFO)

The kernel forcibly interrupts the child thread execution to jump directly into a registered handler.

    Latency: Immediate execution upon kernel delivery.

    Safety: The handler must strictly execute async-signal-safe functions, or simply update atomic flags/futex state to notify a worker thread.

Real-Time Signals vs. eventfd
Feature	POSIX Real-Time Signals	eventfd
Notification Vector	Process-wide or Thread-wide interrupt	File descriptor event
Delivery Mechanism	Kernel signal queue	File descriptor read() or epoll
Data Payload	Integer or Pointer (union sigval)	8-byte counter state
Kernel Overhead	Queue allocation per signal sent	Fixed single-counter file object
Primary Advantage	Can interrupt running user code immediately	Clean integration into select/poll/epoll event loops
