What is the latency on those signals?

On standard Linux kernels running on modern hardware, POSIX Real-Time Signals typically deliver latencies between 1.5 and 4 microseconds when waking a sleeping thread via sigwaitinfo().
Signaling Mechanism	Standard Linux	PREEMPT_RT Kernel	Main Latency Source
Shared Memory Atomic Spin-lock	< 0.1 microseconds	< 0.1 microseconds	CPU bus interconnect (100% CPU utilization)
Futex / Shared Condvar	0.8 – 2.5 microseconds	0.3 – 1.0 microseconds	sys_futex syscall overhead (only when sleeping)
eventfd (write to read/epoll)	1.0 – 3.0 microseconds	0.5 – 1.5 microseconds	Syscall execution & kernel wake-up queue
Real-Time Signal (sigqueue to sigwaitinfo)	1.5 – 4.0 microseconds	0.5 – 2.0 microseconds	Signal queue allocation & kernel dispatch

Key Factors That Impact Real-Time Signal Latency

    Kernel Preemption: Standard Linux kernels introduce jitter (occasionally spiking to 10+ microseconds) because kernel code sections can briefly lock out preemption. A kernel compiled with the PREEMPT_RT patchset enforces hard real-time execution with deterministic bounds.

    CPU Core Affinities (sched_setaffinity): If the host and loaded child thread run on different cores, latency stays low. If they share a core, context-switching overhead adds several microseconds. Pinning threads to isolated cores using isolcpus avoids core sharing entirely.

    CPU C-States / Power Management: If the receiving thread goes to sleep and the CPU core enters a deep power-saving C-state, waking the core back up can add 10 to 100+ microseconds of latency. Low-latency systems set the CPU scaling governor to performance and disable deep C-states (processor.max_cstate=1).

