To achieve sub-microsecond IPC latency for a Linux PLC application, configure kernel core isolation, real-time thread scheduling, memory locking, and CPU power management.

Kernel Boot Parameters
Add the following parameters to your kernel boot line (e.g., in /etc/default/grub under GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT):

    isolcpus=2,3: Prevents the standard OS Linux scheduler from placing processes on cores 2 and 3.

    nohz_full=2,3: Eliminates periodic kernel timer ticks on isolated cores whenever a single real-time thread is executing.

    rcu_nocbs=2,3: Offloads Read-Copy-Update (RCU) callback processing away from isolated cores to housekeeping cores (cores 0 and 1).

    processor.max_cstate=1 intel_idle.max_cstate=0: Disables power-saving C-states to keep CPU cores in active execution states continuously.

    idle=poll: Forces idle execution loops to spin-poll rather than enter sleep states, removing CPU wake-up delays entirely.

    nmi_watchdog=0: Disables Non-Maskable Interrupt watchdog interrupts that periodically interrupt execution.

    clocksource=tsc tsc=reliable: Forces high-resolution Time Stamp Counter reads without dynamic kernel re-calibration overhead.

Application Code Configuration
Setting	Function / System Call	Purpose
Memory Locking	mlockall(MCL_CURRENT | MCL_FUTURE)	Prevents kernel page faults by pinning all current and future virtual memory into physical RAM.
RT Scheduling	pthread_setschedparam() with SCHED_FIFO (Priority 95–99)	Preempts all standard system threads immediately when the RT thread is runnable.
Core Pinning	pthread_setaffinity_np()	Binds the host signaling thread to Core 2 and the loaded process/shim thread to Core 3.

System & Hardware Tuning

    IRQ Affinity: Exclude cores 2 and 3 from handling hardware interrupts by adjusting the bitmask in /proc/irq/default_smp_affinity (e.g., set to 3 so interrupts only target cores 0 and 1).

    BIOS Settings: Disable Hyperthreading (SMT) so physical cores do not share execution units or L1/L2 caches. Set power management to "High Performance / Maximum Performance".

    CPU Frequency Governor: Set the scaling governor to performance using cpupower frequency-set -g performance to prevent dynamic CPU frequency scaling latency spikes.

