Does SIGRT exist on macOS?

No, macOS does not support POSIX Real-Time Signals (SIGRTMIN through SIGRTMAX). Apple's Darwin kernel does not implement the POSIX Real-Time Signals extension (_POSIX_REALTIME_SIGNALS).

Key differences on macOS compared to Linux signal architecture:

    No sigqueue(): The sigqueue() system call is missing entirely, so you cannot pass signal payloads using standard POSIX signal APIs.

    No Signal Queueing: macOS only supports standard BSD signals (signals 1 through 31, such as SIGUSR1 and SIGUSR2). These use simple kernel bitmasks, meaning duplicate signals sent in rapid succession are dropped if the target process hasn't handled the first one.

    No Preemption Guarantees: macOS kernel scheduling does not offer hard real-time guarantees equivalent to Linux's PREEMPT_RT kernel.

Recommended Low-Latency IPC Alternatives on macOS

    kqueue / kevent: The native BSD event notification system (equivalent to Linux epoll). You can trigger custom low-latency user events between processes using EVFILT_USER.

    Mach Ports (mach_port_t): The underlying native Darwin IPC mechanism. Mach messaging provides microsecond-level process-to-process signaling and message passing.

    POSIX Shared Memory + Process-Shared Condition Variables: Combining shm_open() with pthread_cond_t (initialized with PTHREAD_PROCESS_SHARED) works on both macOS and Linux, offering sub-microsecond shared-memory notification when paired with mutexes.

    Unix Domain Sockets (AF_UNIX): macOS supports standard socketpair() and Unix domain sockets, which integrate cleanly with kqueue or poll() event loops.

If cross-platform support between Linux and macOS is required for your host-to-shim architecture, using shared memory with Unix sockets or kqueue/epoll abstraction wrappers provides the most reliable portability.
