How could I best bundle this second shim in my original program? I don't want to use fork()

To bundle a child shim directly inside your main host executable without creating temporary files on disk and without calling manual fork(), combine linker binary embedding, memfd_create, and posix_spawn().

1. Embed the Shim Binary at Build Time
Pack the compiled shim executable into your host binary during compilation so you ship a single executable file:

    objcopy (Standard ELF Embedding): Convert the compiled shim binary into an object file linked directly into the host build:
    Bash

    objcopy -I binary -O elf64-x86-64 -B i386 shim_bin shim_embed.o

    This automatically exposes auto-generated global symbols in C: extern char _binary_shim_bin_start[], _binary_shim_bin_end[], and _binary_shim_bin_size.

    C23 #embed or xxd -i: Convert the binary into a static unsigned char shim_bytes[] array header file included directly in your C/C++ source.

2. Create a Memory File Descriptor (memfd_create)
To execute the shim directly from memory without saving it to /tmp or the filesystem:

    Call int fd = memfd_create("shim_process", MFD_CLOEXEC);.

    Write the embedded byte array (_binary_shim_bin_start) into fd.

    Set execution permissions or seal the descriptor (fcntl(fd, F_ADD_SEALS, F_SEAL_SEAL | F_SEAL_WRITE)).

    The executable path is now accessible via Linux's process file descriptor virtual path: /proc/self/fd/<fd>.

3. Launch the Subprocess Safely (posix_spawn)
Manual fork() in multithreaded real-time PLC hosts is dangerous because it duplicates the calling thread while leaving background thread mutexes locked in an unrecoverable state. posix_spawn() avoids manual fork management and executes cleanly via optimized clone() kernel primitives.

    Pass /proc/self/fd/<fd> as the file path to posix_spawn().

    Use posix_spawn_file_actions_t to map your AF_UNIX socket, eventfd, and memfd descriptors directly into the child process's file descriptor table upon creation.

Stage	Mechanism	Benefit
Storage	objcopy ELF embedding	Single binary deliverable; zero external runtime binary dependencies.
Staging	memfd_create()	Entirely RAM-backed; diskless execution and tamper-proof.
Execution	posix_spawn() via /proc/self/fd/N	Safe in multithreaded hosts; avoids fork() thread lock traps.
