Or is fork() fine here, too? I just think that it might be wasteful to fork off the whole big process just for doing a shim?

fork() is not as memory-wasteful as it seems because Linux uses Copy-on-Write (CoW), but for a real-time host process, it still introduces severe performance and safety drawbacks compared to posix_spawn().
The Memory Reality: Copy-on-Write (CoW)

When you call fork(), the Linux kernel does not copy the physical memory pages of the parent process. It only duplicates the parent’s page table entries and sets both processes' memory pages to read-only. Actual memory pages are only copied if either process mutates data in that memory block.

If you immediately call execve() after fork(), virtually no memory is duplicated.
Why fork() Still Hurts in a Real-Time Host

Despite Copy-on-Write, using fork() to spawn a small shim process introduces three major problems:

    Page Table Copy Overhead: If your host process has allocated large chunks of virtual memory (e.g., gigabytes of buffers or high memory address space), Linux must still duplicate millions of page table entries. This operation locks kernel structures and can cause latency spikes lasting several milliseconds.

    Memory Overcommit Failures (ENOMEM): If your Linux system uses strict memory accounting (vm.overcommit_memory = 2, common on industrial PLCs to avoid the OOM killer), fork() temporarily doubles your process's requested virtual memory allocation. If free memory is lower than your host process's virtual size, fork() will fail with ENOMEM—even though CoW wouldn't actually consume physical RAM.

    Multithreaded Deadlock Traps: In a multithreaded application, calling fork() creates a child containing only the thread that invoked fork(). Any mutexes held by background host threads at that exact microsecond will remain permanently locked in the child process. If the child attempts to call any function that acquires a lock (like malloc() or printf()) prior to exec(), it will deadlock immediately.

Why posix_spawn() Solves This

Under the hood on Linux, posix_spawn() bypasses standard fork() by calling vfork() or clone() with the CLONE_VM and CLONE_VFORK flags.

This forces the child process to execute directly within the parent's address space without duplicating page tables or checking overcommit limits. The parent process is briefly suspended until the child completes the execve() execution call, making the spawn operation near-instantaneous and thread-safe.
