Could I also fork() and immediately drop most of the stuff and "shirink" my binary down?

No, calling fork() alone cannot "shrink" or replace the host binary image. While you can free heap memory or unmap data regions, the child process still retains the host's entire executable code segment (.text), symbol tables, and virtual address space.

    Code Segment Retention: The kernel marks the executable code of the parent process as read-only and shares it with the child. You cannot unmap the code segment of the executable currently running the process.

    Process Identity: /proc/self/exe continues to point to the large host binary on disk, meaning security scanners, system monitors, and the OS still treat the child as an instance of the host.

    Memory Metadata Overhead: Even if you call free() on dynamic memory allocations or munmap() on mapped regions, the internal allocator metadata and thread descriptors created by the host remain in memory.

The Only Way to Truly "Shrink": execve() / fexecve()

To strip away the host process completely and reduce the footprint down to just the tiny shim executable, you must replace the process image using the exec family of system calls:

    fork() or vfork() / posix_spawn(): Creates the child process shell.

    execve() or fexecve(): The kernel completely wipes the existing address space—destroying the host's stack, heap, thread mappings, and code segments—and loads the lean shim binary into its place.

If you use memfd_create() to hold your embedded shim binary in RAM, you can pass its file descriptor directly to fexecve(fd, argv, envp). This completely replaces the host executable with the shim in memory without touching the disk.
