Rust: Could I somehow achieve a bundled shim program? Like build the shim as a dependency and include it in the host binary?

Yes, you can build the shim automatically during compilation and embed its raw executable bytes directly into your host binary using a Cargo workspace, a build script (build.rs), and the include_bytes! macro.

1. Project Workspace Setup

Structure your project as a Cargo workspace with two separate packages: host and shim.
Plaintext

my_project/
├── Cargo.toml      # Workspace manifest
├── host/
│   ├── Cargo.toml
│   ├── build.rs    # Compiles the shim before host builds
│   └── src/
│       └── main.rs
└── shim/
    ├── Cargo.toml
    └── src/
        └── main.rs

Workspace Manifest (Cargo.toml):
Ini, TOML

[workspace]
members = ["host", "shim"]

2. Automate Shim Compilation in build.rs

In host/build.rs, invoke cargo to build the shim crate in release mode and place the output binary inside Cargo's OUT_DIR.
Rust

use std::env;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::process::Command;

fn main() {
    // Re-run this build script if any source in the shim package changes
    println!("cargo:rerun-if-changed=../shim/src");

    let out_dir = PathBuf::from(env::var("OUT_DIR").unwrap());

    // Build the shim binary targeting the out_dir
    let status = Command::new(env::var("CARGO").unwrap_or_else(|_| "cargo".into()))
        .args([
            "build",
            "--package",
            "shim",
            "--release",
            "--target-dir",
        ])
        .arg(&out_dir)
        .status()
        .expect("Failed to execute cargo build for shim");

    assert!(status.success(), "Failed to build shim binary");

    // Copy or locate the resulting binary artifact
    let shim_bin_path = out_dir.join("release").join("shim");
    let dest_path = out_dir.join("shim_embedded");

    std::fs::copy(&shim_bin_path, &dest_path)
        .expect("Failed to copy shim binary to OUT_DIR");
}

3. Embed and Execute In-Memory in host/src/main.rs

Use include_bytes! to pull the compiled shim binary directly into the host executable at compile time. Then write it to an in-memory file descriptor (memfd_create) and execute it via /proc/self/fd/N using Rust's standard Command API (which uses posix_spawn/vfork under the hood on Linux).
Rust

use std::fs::File;
use std::io::Write;
use std::os::fd::AsRawFd;
use std::process::Command;

// Embed the binary compiled by build.rs into read-only memory
static SHIM_BYTES: &[u8] = include_bytes!(concat!(env!("OUT_DIR"), "/shim_embedded"));

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // 1. Create anonymous in-memory file descriptor
    let memfd_name = std::ffi::CString::new("shim_process")?;
    let raw_fd = unsafe {
        libc::memfd_create(memfd_name.as_ptr(), libc::MFD_CLOEXEC)
    };
    if raw_fd < 0 {
        return Err("memfd_create failed".into());
    }

    let mut mem_file = unsafe { File::from_raw_fd(raw_fd) };

    // 2. Write embedded bytes into the memory file
    mem_file.write_all(SHIM_BYTES)?;

    // 3. Spawn child process directly from RAM via /proc/self/fd
    let proc_path = format!("/proc/self/fd/{}", raw_fd);
    let mut child = Command::new(proc_path)
        .arg("--parent-pid")
        .arg(std::process::id().to_string())
        .spawn()?;

    println!("Spawned embedded shim process (PID: {})", child.id());

    // Wait or manage child process...
    child.wait()?;
    Ok(())
}

